%% DM26-0670

%% A pod gets its certificates, as built (2026-11-04, docs/architecture/key-custody.md "Part A's bootstrap: the custodian", deployments.md "The renewal rule",
%% credentials-agent-architecture.md). What the credentials agent asks of the attestation agent is general/attestation_instance_check. The bootstrap
%% services (Cassini, the credentials agent and its observer) ask cert-issuer directly, on their token: general/core_restart_ticket.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Init as polar-init (init container)
    participant Daemon as custody daemon (a sidecar)
    participant Obs as Credentials observer<br/>(low trust)
    participant CA as Credentials agent<br/>(consumer)
    participant Att as Attestation agent
    participant CI as cert-issuer
    participant Bk as Cassini broker
    participant Agent as the workload's container

    Init->>Init: judge the environment and --cert<br/>arguments as a whole, then wait for<br/>the dependencies' TLS endpoints
    Init->>Init: read the projected token,<br/>and from its sub the identity X
    Note over Init,Daemon: One UID, so nothing is decided by what the daemon is told over its socket. A bundle a run before<br/>this one left in this pod, still good, is kept: a restart in place asks only for what it lacks.
    Init->>Daemon: the custodian slot's CSR. The daemon made the key, and no request signs with that slot

    Note over Init,Obs: The token is not on the pod network in the clear, and the observer never holds it. A certificate whose<br/>key is polar-init's own, not the daemon's, is asked for this same way, first, and written with its key.
    Init->>Obs: POST /workload/session
    Obs->>CA: a session, over Cassini
    CA-->>Init: SessionOffer(id, public key, lifetime), signed, through the observer
    Init->>Init: the signature holds under the pinned CA,<br/>a signing certificate, naming the credentials<br/>agent. Only then seal
    Init->>Obs: POST /workload/issue(session, sealed token, Custody, the custodian's CSR)
    Obs->>CA: the sealed bytes, over Cassini
    CA->>CA: open the seal, verify the token: its identity, and the pod UID it is bound to
    CA->>Att: the instance check. No answer, no session, or CannotSay: issued and recorded IssuedUnattested
    Att-->>CA: Genuine, or NotGenuine: refused, and the pod restarts in place and asks again
    CA->>CA: the renewal rule, per identity, pod and type: not issued one of that type within five minutes
    CA->>CI: /internal/issue: names from the catalog, the p: names say what the custodian may fetch
    CI-->>CA: custody.X's certificate
    CA-->>Init: it, through the observer
    Init->>Init: signed by the pinned CA, for this<br/>workload? Otherwise it is refused
    Init->>Daemon: install it, and the slot is sealed. The token has bought this and nothing else

    rect rgb(250, 240, 215)
    Note over Daemon,Bk: The custodian fetches the rest, over a Cassini session of its own, as custody.X. No token travels.
    Daemon->>Bk: register as custody.X, and ask for the authority snapshot
    Daemon->>CA: for each slot its certificate names: the slot's key and CSR
    CA->>CA: only to custody.X, only what its certificate names, once each, within five minutes of its issue
    CA->>CI: /internal/issue
    CI-->>CA: X's client or server certificate, no longer-lived than the custodian's
    CA-->>Daemon: it
    opt the workload has encryption
        Daemon->>CA: X's escrow key, sealed to an ephemeral the daemon opens itself
        alt one is escrowed
            CA-->>Daemon: it, sealed to the ephemeral
        else none: this pod is the first
            Daemon->>Daemon: make a P-256 key
            Daemon->>CA: have it issued, then store it, sealed to the credentials agent's pinned key
            CA-->>Daemon: its escrow certificate (another pod meanwhile is told BeingIssued, and asks again)
        end
        Note over CA,Daemon: Refused to X itself: the workload can neither retrieve the key nor replace it.
    end
    end

    Init->>Daemon: Ready? Asked until it is, for at most 240 s, inside its five minutes
    Daemon-->>Init: ready
    Init->>Daemon: Certificate, for each slot
    Init->>Init: write the public bundle<br/>(certificates and ca.pem), and no key
    Init->>Init: leave the one-use start marker
    Init->>Agent: exit 0: the workload's container starts, takes the marker, and asks its daemon to sign
    Note over Daemon: Its authority is spent. What it keeps is a certificate good for listening: authority updates, and its own revocation.
    Note over Init,Agent: Nothing renews. The workload's health actor ends it as its shortest-lived certificate nears expiry, and the pod restarts in place with empty slots.
